## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to design a CKNE-driven throttling system across a real-time, multi-tenant telemetry path, balancing SLA commitments with global health.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE health propagation across hops
- Per-tenant priority and budget management
- Minute-by-minute degradation policy and backpressure
- Payload schemas and validation
- Testing for bursts and SLA adherence

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo throttle by CKNE health (high level, not production-ready)
function throttleByCKNE(tenantId, ckne) {
  const bucket = getTenantBucket(tenantId);
  if (ckne.health < 0.5) bucket.reduce(ckne.sampling);
  // return true to emit, false to drop
  return Math.random() < bucket.sampling;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate correctness under micro-bursts with minimal data loss?
- How would you adapt this for multi-region deployments with cross-region CKNE signals?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses CKNE-driven multi-tenant graph analytics design, focusing on per-tenant prioritization, adaptive traversal, caching, and safe degradation under load. Requires concrete parameters, fairness guarantees, observability hooks, and a test plan.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-routing and depth mapping
- Backpressure and resource reallocation
- Incremental caching and warm/cold budgets
- Telemetry, SLAs, and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
function adjustForCKNE(tenantCKNE, req) {
  const baseDepth = 6;
  if (tenantCKNE < 0.3) {
    req.depth = Math.min(req.depth || baseDepth, 3);
    req.rateLimit = Math.min(req.rateLimit || 200, 100);
  } else if (tenantCKNE > 0.7) {
    req.depth = Math.max(req.depth || baseDepth, 8);
    req.cacheHint = 'warm';
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the degradation loop under bursty CKNE distributions?
- How would you ensure fairness when CKNE changes dynamically?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical CKNE-to-quotas mapping, token-based fairness, and SLA guarantees in a real-world multi-tenant dispatch scenario.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE-to-quotas mapping
- Token-bucket with a global minute cap
- Minute-by-minute reallocation and carryover
- SLA baseline with priority to high-CKNE tenants

## Code Example
```javascript
function allocateQuotas(totalCap, tenants) {
  const sumCKNE = tenants.reduce((s,t)=>s+t.ckne,0) || 1;
  return tenants.map(t => {
    const q = Math.floor(totalCap * (t.ckne / sumCKNE));
    return {tenant: t.tenant, quota: Math.max(1, q)};
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle new tenants joining or CKNE updates during a minute?
- What telemetry would you collect to verify fairness and SLA adherence?

## Why This Is Asked
CKNE-aware caching for multi-tenant real-time pipelines is a plausible, high-impact reliability topic that blends performance with health signals.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant TTL policy driven by CKNE health
- Cache stampede guards with jitter and request coalescing
- Edge prefetch/invalidation triggered by health trend

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal TTL decision helper
function ttlForTenant(baseTTL, ckne) {
  const health = ckne?.health ?? 1;
  const factor = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, health));
  const ttl = Math.round(baseTTL * (1 - (1 - factor) * 0.5));
  return Math.max(30, Math.min(3600, ttl));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test TTL drift under bursts?
- How would you handle new tenants with no CKNE history?
